          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %strong group scheme
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+object"} group object
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} in
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/schemes"} schemes
            (or in a category of
            %em some
            schemes as for instance that of schemes over a fixed base scheme); in particular a group scheme is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+functor"} group functor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+object"} group object
            there are two equivalent ways of realizing this:
          %p
            One way is to define it as a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mi Ring
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G:C Ring\to Set
            equipped with a transformation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo :
                  %mi G
                  %mo ×
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m:G\times G\to G
            satisfying the properties spelled out at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+object"} group object
          %p
            The other way is to define it as a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sch
                  %mo →
                  %mi Grp
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sch\to Grp
            from the category of schemes to that of (discrete) groups whose composition with the forgetful functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Grp
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Grp\to Set
            is representable.
          %p
            Grothendieck emphasized the study of schemes over a fixed base scheme. Following this idea in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+of+points"} functor of points
            formalism, a group scheme over a scheme
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Sch
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi X
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Grp
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  G: (Sch /X)^{op} \to Grp
          %p
            A
            %strong morphism of group schemes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
